ly in some countries  this variant as we talked about last  week with dr rasheesh  jah this is a variant that not only  spread 60  more quickly it's becoming dominant if  we look at cases in the united  kingdom cases are going up  now this is not cases are going down but  more slowly  we are actually seeing an increase in  cases in the united kingdom they were  down to  under 2 000 new cases a day they're now  back up to almost 10  000 cases a day now you might see this  and say david this is no problem you  know why  because in the u.s we've vaccinated way  more people than in the united kingdom  so we don't have to worry and you would  be  100 wrong if you look at vaccination  rates by country  you see that the u.s has fully  vaccinated 45 percent of our population  and the uk has fully vaccinated more 47  of its population it is wrong to say we  don't have to worry about  delta because we vaccinated more people  we haven't  what's happening is that the delta  variant  is dominant in the uk already and in the  u.s it is not yet  dominant this variant started in india  60 of new cases in the uk are now the  delta variant  only 10 of new cases in the u.s are now  the delta variant  so what that means is we are simply  on a delay from what is happening in the  united kingdom now  let's be really really clear if you were  vaccinated  at least with the vaccines approved here  here pfizer  moderna or johnson and johnson you are  protected against the delta variant the  vaccines work  really well against the delta variant  the problem  is that 55 percent of the country has no  protection right now  and so when you say well our pool of  vaccinated people is half the country  and this variant spreads sixty percent  more easily  you are going to get more and more cases  among the unvaccinated but you will also  get more cases among the vaccinated  because  vaccination is not 100 it's just simple  math  if you have a vaccine that's 95  effective  when there aren't many cases